**What I added** — one subtest, `cmd/bd/export_test.go:498`, `TestClaimRoundTrip/rename collision keeps the lease on the surviving ID`. Reaching `handleRename`'s update map needs two seeded rows in the target store, not one: a *twin* holding the incoming content under a different ID of the same `test-` prefix (this is what makes the importer read the row as a rename, since content hash excludes `claim_expires_at`), plus a *squatter* holding the incoming ID with different content (this is what makes `handleRename` keep the twin's ID and merge the incoming fields in via the field-by-field map instead of delete-and-recreate). A single seed would have fallen through to `CreateIssue`, which carries the lease through the INSERT column list and would have proved nothing. The test then asserts status, assignee, and lease on the twin's ID after import.

**Mutation check** — with the subtest in place I deleted `"claim_expires_at": incoming.ClaimExpiresAt` from the `handleRename` map at `internal/importer/importer.go:384`. The new subtest failed (`rename collision: claim_expires_at is NULL after import, want 2026-08-07 19:28:50...`) while the other three still passed, which is exactly the gap the review reported. I restored the line with `git checkout --`, so `internal/importer/importer.go` is untouched in the commit; the only changed file is `cmd/bd/export_test.go` (+42).

